<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Bakr al-Razi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 864–925 or 935</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Abū Bakr al-Rāzī, Rhazes, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Zakariyyāʾ al-Rāzī</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abū Bakr al-Rāzī, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> as Rhazes, was a Persian Muslim physician, philosopher and alchemist of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-golden-age">Islamic Golden Age</a>, born in the city of Ray. He studied medicine in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and served as chief physician of hospitals in Baghdad and Ray, and with Thābit ibn Qurra he was among the first physicians to distinguish clinically between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/measles">measles</a>. He is credited with more than 200 works; his medical encyclopaedia Ḥāwī (Continens), completed by his pupils, was translated into Latin in 1279 and printed five times between 1488 and 1542, shaping medieval European medicine. In philosophy he gave reason a high place and was critical of prophethood and revelation.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-al-razi.jpg" alt="Abu Bakr al-Razi" width="330" height="375" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerard of Cremona · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Al-RaziInGerardusCremonensis1250.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
